BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Roots of Health | The 12th Annual Public Health Speaker Series
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261015T170000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261015T190000
UID:261008lDiulGC6tUuIpPfjHXR91A
DESCRIPTION:Our 12th Annual Touro University California Public Health Spea
 ker Series continues its tradition of bringing together experts\, scholars
 \, and community leaders to explore timely issues shaping public health an
 d health disparities. This year’s series looks upstream to examine the h
 istorical\, economic\, and structural forces that shape health and well-be
 ing. Speakers will explore themes including: - History and its lasting imp
 act on health\, including how past policies and systems continue to influe
 nce present-day disparities - Poverty\, income\, and economic inequality a
 s fundamental drivers of health and opportunity - Structural factors and p
 ublic health\, examining how systems\, policies\, and institutions shape t
 he conditions in which people live\, work\, and thrive Together\, the seri
 es will examine how historical\, economic\, and institutional systems prod
 uce and sustain unequal health outcomes and consider the policy and system
 ic changes needed to improve health and well-being across communities. Lec
 tures are open to the public and free! Light refreshments are provided\, a
 nd registration is required.  Visit this link to RSVP today: https://www.e
 ventbrite.com/e/12th-annual-public-health-speaker-series-2026-tickets-1999
 532935837?aff=oddtdtcreator Registration to join virtually: https://tourou
 niversity.zoom.us/webinar/register/WN\\_pa1biV8OSpCeRy5iYKeckg\\#/registra
 tion FULL SERIES SCHEDULE: September 24: Immigration\, Social Movements\, 
 and Poverty: A Global Perspective – Genevieve Negrón-Gonzales\, PhD An 
 examination of immigration\, education\, economic conditions\, and social 
 policy\, and how these forces influence opportunity\, community well-being
 \, and health. October 15: Native Communities\, History\, and Economic Wel
 l-Being – Cutcha Risling Baldy\, PhD A historical perspective on the eco
 nomic and social conditions that have shaped the health and well-being of 
 Native communities\, including the continuing influence of policy\, land\,
  education\, and community resources. October 29: Children\, Poverty\, and
  Public Policy – Shimica Gaskins\, JD A policy and advocacy perspective 
 on the economic and social conditions affecting children and families\, wi
 th attention to how public policy can influence opportunity\, health\, and
  community well-being. November 12: Poverty\, Incarceration\, and Economic
  Stability – Reuben Jonathan Miller\, PhD An examination of the relation
 ship between poverty and incarceration and how involvement with the crimin
 al legal system can affect employment\, housing\, family economic stabilit
 y\, and health. November 19: Local Perspectives on Homelessness and the Un
 housed: Poverty\, Economic Insecurity\, and Public Health - Panel Panelist
 s: TBD A public health perspective on homelessness and the experiences of 
 unhoused individuals and families in our local communities\, with attentio
 n to the economic and structural conditions that contribute to housing ins
 tability and homelessness\, the health consequences of being unhoused\, an
 d the challenges people face in accessing health care\, housing\, social s
 ervices\, and other supports. December 3 – Poverty\, Food Insecurity\, a
 nd Community Solutions – Lisa Goldman Rosas\, PhD An examination of the 
 relationship between poverty\, access to nutritious food\, and health\, wi
 th attention to public policy\, community programs\, and practical approac
 hes to improving food access.
LOCATION:Lander Hall B\, 105 Owen Drive
URL:https://www.facebook.com/events/1750505649493211/1750505662826543/?act
 ive_tab=about
X-SOURCE:North Bay Bohemian
END:VEVENT
